(window.__D=window.__D||{})["data/ch07.json"]={"n": 7, "title": "The Ottoman centuries (1461–1800)", "events": [{"id": "H5-001", "date": "1461", "date_end": "1463", "date_text": "After the surrender in summer 1461 (traditionally 15 August); David Komnenos executed 1 November 1463", "precision": "year", "title": "Mehmed II dismantles the Empire of Trebizond", "summary": "After the surrender, Mehmed II shipped David Komnenos, his court and leading families to Constantinople, put a Janissary garrison of 400 in the citadel and named Kasım Pasha governor. Kritoboulos says 1,500 children of both sexes were taken as the sultan's slaves; the continuator of Chalkokondyles says the townspeople were split into three groups (resettled in Constantinople, enslaved, or expelled to the countryside) and 800 boys were taken for the Janissaries. Bryer found signs that rural groups resisted for up to ten years. The deportations explain why the first Ottoman registers show a small city. Chrysanthos (1933, pp. 516-519) follows the continuator of Chalkokondyles and Doukas: the three-way division, 800 boys for the janissaries, and the remaining third settled outside the walls in the parishes of St Sophia and St Philip. Lowry, reading the first register (ca. 1486), argues against the picture, from Fallmerayer and Finlay, of a city emptied of Greeks and closed to Christians: in ca. 1486 Greek households still lived inside the walls (the Meso Kastro quarter), and the early Muslims were mostly deportees from central Anatolia, 202 households from nineteen towns (Niksar, Amasya, Tokat, Samsun, Bafra and others), 78.3% of the permanent Muslim population.", "people": ["Mehmed II", "David Komnenos", "Kasım Pasha", "David Megas Komnenos"], "sources_primary": ["Kritoboulos, History IV.46-52 (IV.48 leading men moved; IV.49 1,500 children; IV.50 garrison of 400)", "Pseudo-Chalkokondyles, Histories 9.76-79 (9.79 three groups, 800 boys)", "Doukas 45.19", "Kritoboulos IV.48-50 (trans. Riggs 1954, p. 175): only 'the most influential men' moved to Constantinople (IV.48); 1,500 children (IV.49); 400 janissaries in the citadel (IV.50)", "Pseudo-Chalkokondyles (continuator) 9.79 (trans. Kaldellis 2014, vol. II, p. 365): 800 boys to the janissaries; three groups of deportees", "Ottoman tax register of 1486 (Lowry)", "George Amiroutzes, letter to Bessarion after the fall (PG 161, 723-728; ed. Boissonade, Anecdota graeca, pp. 389-401)"], "sources_modern": ["Lowry 2009, The Islamization & Turkification of the City of Trabzon (Trebizond), 1461–1583, pp. 5–9 (Finlay, Fallmerayer), p. 28 (the 202 deported households, Table I p. 30)", "Lowry 2009, ch. I", "Bryer 1980 (Variorum), on rural resistance", "Chrysanthos 1933, Η Εκκλησία Τραπεζούντος (Αρχείον Πόντου 4–5), pp.516–519; p.531", "Lowry 1981 and 2009, The Islamization and Turkification of the City of Trabzon (Trebizond), 1461-1583", "Babinger 1978", "Topalidis 2024", "Chrysanthos 1933 (Chrysanthos Philippides, Ἡ Ἐκκλησία Τραπεζοῦντος, Ἀρχεῖον Πόντου 4–5, Athens 1933), pp. 515–519; p. 516", "Malone-Lee 2023, pp. 12-13 (Amiroutzes' letter, PG 161, cols 723-728)", "Cattaneo 2020, pp. 17-18 n. 38 (the letter, ed. Boissonade, Anecdota graeca, pp. 389-401; Lampsides, 'Πῶς ἠλώθη')"], "confidence": "high", "disputed": "The numbers are chroniclers' and differ: Kritoboulos, who wrote for Mehmed and plays the violence down, has 1,500 children taken and only the leading men deported; the continuator of Chalkokondyles a third of the population deported and 800 boys taken. Neither is a count. Lowry's registers show the city still mostly Christian about 1486, so the deportation took the elite rather than the whole population.", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Trapezous (Trabzon), citadel", "lat": 41.005, "lon": 39.722, "role": "surrendered city; garrison"}, {"name": "Constantinople (Istanbul)", "lat": 41.008, "lon": 28.978, "role": "destination of the court and deportees"}, {"name": "Trapezous (Trebizond, Trabzon)", "lat": 41.005, "lon": 39.73, "role": "waypoint 1 (origin)"}, {"name": "Constantinople", "lat": 41.008, "lon": 28.978, "role": "waypoint 2 (destination of the deportees)"}]}, {"id": "H5-002", "date": "1461", "date_end": "1553", "date_text": "1461-1553", "precision": "century", "title": "Matsouka's Christian sipahis and the monasteries' privileges", "summary": "In Matsouka (Maçka), the valley of Soumela, Vazelon and Peristera, the Ottomans kept Christian timar-holders (sipahis) from the old Trapezuntine landholders for decades. They also confirmed monastic property by firman. The three monasteries held land and villages and kept them through the Ottoman centuries. They later ranked as patriarchal exarchates; an archbishopric of Rhodopolis replaced them in 1863-1867 (H5-038), and they lasted until the metropolis of Rhodopolis was created in October 1902 (C3-030).", "sources_primary": ["Tahrir defters of 1486-1553 (Maçka)", "Ottoman firmans in the monastery archives (published in Αρχείον Πόντου)"], "sources_modern": ["Lowry 1986, 'Privilege and property in Ottoman Maçuka in the opening decades of the Tourkokratia, 1461-1553', in Bryer & Lowry eds. 1986", "Bryer & Winfield 1985"], "confidence": "medium", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Maçka (Matsouka)", "lat": 40.813, "lon": 39.612, "role": "valley"}, {"name": "Soumela monastery", "lat": 40.69, "lon": 39.658, "role": "monastery"}, {"name": "Vazelon monastery", "lat": 40.74, "lon": 39.59, "role": "monastery (coordinates approximate)"}, {"name": "Peristera monastery (Kuştul)", "lat": 40.884, "lon": 39.8, "role": "monastery (coordinates approximate)"}]}, {"id": "C3-003", "date": "1461", "date_end": "1923", "date_text": "From the conquest to the end of the community", "precision": "century", "title": "Two bishoprics and an ethnarchy: the Church after 1461", "summary": "Chrysanthos writes that after 1461 'ἐσβέσθησαν αἱ πρότερον ἀκμάζουσαι 11 ἐπισκοπαί' (the eleven formerly flourishing bishoprics died out), and that only two were formed from their remnants: Of, and Kanis (Chaldia and Cheriana). The metropolitan was recognised as millet başı, with autonomous church courts and a prison, 'κράτος ἐν κράτει' (a state within the state). Muslims and Armenians often took disputes with Orthodox to the metropolis, and during the Russian occupation of 1916–18 Muslims took even disputes among themselves there. Villages were run by an elected muhtar and azades; the community of Trebizond had nine parishes, a demogerontia and later an elected assembly.", "sources_modern": ["Chrysanthos 1933, Η Εκκλησία Τραπεζούντος (Αρχείον Πόντου 4–5), pp.529–530; pp.673–680"], "confidence": "medium", "disputed": "'A state within the state' describes the late Ottoman millet; the use of the metropolis' court by Muslims in 1916–18 rests on Chrysanthos' word.", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Trapezous (Trabzon)", "lat": 41.0024, "lon": 39.7189, "role": "seat of the metropolis"}, {"name": "Of", "lat": 40.946, "lon": 40.27, "role": "bishopric of Of (extinguished in the mid-17th century)"}, {"name": "Argyroupolis (Gümüşhane, old town)", "lat": 40.4598, "lon": 39.4778, "role": "bishopric of Kanis (Chaldia and Cheriana)"}]}, {"id": "C3-002", "date": "1472", "date_text": "1472", "precision": "year", "title": "Metropolitan Pankratios removed by order of Mehmed II", "summary": "Pankratios, ordained in 1472 by Patriarch Symeon I (himself a Trapezuntine), was in the same year 'ἐξηλάθη ἀπὸ τοῦ θρόνου' (driven from his throne) 'διὰ θέλησιν καὶ βούλησιν αὐθεντικήν' (by the sovereign's will and pleasure) and made to resign in writing. A sultanic order demanded a quick successor, and Dorotheos of Athens was transferred with the title 'ἔξαρχος πάσης Λαζικῆς' (exarch of all Lazike). Chrysanthos links the expulsion 'πιθανῶς' (probably) to Uzun Hasan's campaign of 1472 to restore Trebizond, which David's nephew Alexios joined. He dates to 1472–73 the folk traditions of castles holding out in the hills (the Helios castle at the sources of the Sera, 'τῆς Ὡριᾶς τὸ κάστρον', Petroma, the Palaiokastron of Palaiomatsouka), to which Trapezuntines had fled from the city. Chrysanthos also says that after 1461 the seat had moved to the church of St Philip, that two bishoprics (Ophis; Kanis, i.e. Chaldia and Cheriana) remained, and that Pankratios, who had succeeded 'probably the metropolitan Dorotheos', was followed in the same year by Dorotheos II from Athens.", "people": ["Pankratios", "Symeon I", "Mehmed II", "Dorotheos II (of Athens)", "Uzun Hasan", "Alexios (nephew of David Komnenos)"], "sources_primary": ["Patriarchal acts on Pankratios' resignation and Dorotheos' transfer, as printed by Chrysanthos", "Pontic folk traditions and songs, as collected by Chrysanthos", "Patriarchal act for Dorotheos II (1472), as cited by Chrysanthos"], "sources_modern": ["Chrysanthos 1933, Η Εκκλησία Τραπεζούντος (Αρχείον Πόντου 4–5), pp.531–539", "Chrysanthos 1933 (Chrysanthos Philippides, Ἡ Ἐκκλησία Τραπεζοῦντος, Ἀρχεῖον Πόντου 4–5, Athens 1933), pp. 529–533; p. 541"], "confidence": "medium", "disputed": "The link with Uzun Hasan's 1472 campaign, and the dating of the castle traditions to 1472–73 rather than 1461, are Chrysanthos' inferences.", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Trapezous (Trabzon)", "lat": 41.0024, "lon": 39.7189, "role": "see of Pankratios"}, {"name": "Constantinople (Istanbul)", "lat": 41.008, "lon": 28.978, "role": "Patriarchate; the sultan's order"}, {"name": "Matsouka (Maçka)", "lat": 40.8111, "lon": 39.6132, "role": "Matsouka highlands: refuge and castle traditions"}, {"name": "Argyroupolis (Gümüşhane, old town)", "lat": 40.4598, "lon": 39.4778, "role": "Chaldia highlands: refuge"}]}, {"id": "H5-003", "date": "1486", "date_text": "C. 1486 (first surviving detailed register of the city)", "precision": "year", "title": "The first Ottoman tax register of Trabzon city", "summary": "The earliest surviving detailed tahrir defter of the city lists households by quarter (mahalle) and by community: Muslims, Greek Orthodox, Armenians and a small Catholic 'Efrenciyan' group (Genoese and Venetian remnants). Lowry calls the city of c. 1486 'predominately Christian'. Its Muslims were mostly the garrison, officials and settlers moved in by sürgün (forced resettlement). Converts were still few. Lowry's tables: ca. 1486 the city had 258 Muslim households, 749 Greek Orthodox (plus 157 widows and 50 unmarried men), 130 Armenian and 34 Latin; about 6,711 residents by his reckoning (households × 5, widows × 4). Muslims were 19.2% of the population.", "people": ["Bayezid II"], "sources_primary": ["Tahrir defter of Trabzon, c. 1486 (Istanbul, Başbakanlık Osmanlı Arşivi; see Lowry for the shelf mark)"], "sources_modern": ["Lowry 2009, The Islamization & Turkification of the City of Trabzon (Trebizond), 1461–1583, Tables I–II (ca. 1486), pp. 30, 38", "Lowry 1977 (UCLA PhD); Lowry 1981 (Turkish ed.); Lowry 2009, ch. II", "Lowry 1976-77, 'The question of Trabzon's Efrenciyan population', Boğaziçi Üniversitesi Dergisi 4-5, 103-110"], "confidence": "high", "disputed": "The registers count tax-paying households, not people, and Lowry's multipliers (five to a household, four for a widow) are conventions.", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Trabzon (Trapezous)", "lat": 41.005, "lon": 39.726, "role": "registered city"}]}, {"id": "C3-L03", "date": "1486", "date_end": "1523", "date_text": "Between ca. 1486 and ca. 1523", "precision": "decade", "title": "St Eugenios becomes the \"New Mosque\" (Câmi-i Cedid)", "summary": "The register of ca. 1523 lists a Muslim quarter of Câmi-i Cedid (\"the new mosque\") in the eastern suburb, the converted church of St Eugenios, patron saint of the city; it is not yet in the register of ca. 1486. The Chrysokephalos had been turned into the Câmi-i Atik (\"old mosque\", later Ortahisar mosque) at the conquest.", "sources_primary": ["Ottoman registers ca. 1486 (MM 828) and ca. 1523 (TT 387), as read by Lowry"], "sources_modern": ["Lowry 2009, The Islamization & Turkification of the City of Trabzon (Trebizond), 1461–1583, p. 62 (Table VII, ca. 1523)"], "confidence": "medium", "disputed": "Chrysanthos puts the conversion of St Eugenios at the conquest; the registers show the Muslim quarter only by about 1523.", "places": [{"name": "St Eugenios (Yeni Cuma Camii), Trabzon", "lat": 41.0042, "lon": 39.7262, "role": "church converted"}]}, {"id": "H5-004", "date": "1487", "date_end": "1510", "date_text": "1487-1510 (start sometimes given as 1481)", "precision": "year", "title": "Prince Selim governor of Trabzon", "summary": "Bayezid II sent his son Selim to govern the sancak of Trabzon, a frontier province facing the Georgians and, after 1501, the Safavids. From Trabzon Selim built a reputation as a fighting prince. That reputation carried him to the throne in 1512 as Selim I.", "people": ["Selim (later Selim I)", "Bayezid II", "Ayşe Gülbahar Hatun", "Hafsa Hatun"], "sources_primary": ["Ottoman chronicles (Kemalpaşazade; Celalzade)"], "sources_modern": ["Mikhail 2020, God's Shadow"], "confidence": "high", "disputed": "The start date varies between 1481 and 1487 in the literature.", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Trabzon", "lat": 41.005, "lon": 39.726, "role": "seat of the sancakbey"}]}, {"id": "H5-005", "date": "1494-11-06", "date_text": "6 November 1494 (another tradition: 27 April 1495)", "precision": "day", "title": "Süleyman born at Trabzon", "summary": "The future Süleyman the Magnificent was born at Trabzon to Selim and Hafsa Hatun while his father was governor there. A house in the city is shown as his birthplace, but the identification is late tradition.", "people": ["Süleyman I", "Selim I", "Hafsa Hatun"], "sources_primary": ["Ottoman chronicles"], "sources_modern": ["Usta 2019, on the birth-house (cited by Wikipedia)"], "confidence": "high", "disputed": "The day of birth is given as 6 November 1494 or 27 April 1495.", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Trabzon", "lat": 41.005, "lon": 39.726, "role": "birthplace"}]}, {"id": "H5-006", "date": "1505", "date_end": "1510", "date_text": "1505-1510", "precision": "year", "title": "Selim's wars from Trabzon against the Safavids and Georgia", "summary": "In 1505 Selim drove a Safavid raid under Ebrahim Mirza back from Trabzon to Erzincan. In 1507 he attacked Safavid-held Erzincan and Bayburt. In 1508 he invaded western Georgia (May–June 1508 in Kemalpaşazade; the Georgian tradition gives 1509, 1510 or 1512); a figure of more than 10,000 captives appears in modern accounts but has not been traced to a source. In 1510 he beat another Safavid force sent against Trabzon. These campaigns tied the Pontic hinterland (Chaldia, Bayburt) firmly to the Ottomans.", "people": ["Selim", "Ebrahim Mirza", "Shah Ismail I"], "sources_primary": ["Kemalpaşazade (İbn Kemal), Tevârih-i Âl-i Osman (May–June 1508); Vakhushti Batonishvili (1509, 1512): both as reported by Güldür 2021"], "sources_modern": ["Mikhail 2020", "Güldür, K. 2021, PhD thesis summary, Batumi Shota Rustaveli State University: relations of Imereti and the Ottoman Empire, 16th–18th c. (bsu.edu.ge/text_files/en_file_2440_2.pdf)", "Emecen, F., 'Selim I', TDV İslâm Ansiklopedisi (updated 2020): victory over the Georgian king in 914/1508; Bayburt and İspir taken; a Safavid force of 10,000 defeated near Erzincan; no captive figure [Turkish]"], "confidence": "medium", "disputed": "The figure of over 10,000 captives comes from a single modern summary.", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Trabzon", "lat": 41.005, "lon": 39.726, "role": "base"}, {"name": "Bayburt", "lat": 40.255, "lon": 40.225, "role": "attacked 1507"}, {"name": "Erzincan", "lat": 39.75, "lon": 39.5, "role": "Safavid army defeated 1507"}, {"name": "Kutaisi (Imereti)", "lat": 42.27, "lon": 42.7, "role": "western Georgia raided 1508"}]}, {"id": "C3-004", "date": "1512", "date_end": "1902-10", "date_text": "Selim I's confirmation (by tradition); exarchates shaped in the 17th-18th centuries; Paisios' grant of 8 November 1707; suspended 1863-1867 (archbishopric of Rhodopolis), restored 1867 and confirmed 1876; ended with the metropolis of Rhodopolis, October 1902", "precision": "century", "title": "Monastic privileges and the exarchates of Soumela, Vazelon and Peristereota", "summary": "Chrysanthos writes that sultans and Moldo-Wallachian princes endowed Soumela by firmans and chrysobulls, and patriarchs by sigillia, and that by monastic tradition Selim I confirmed by hatt-ı şerif the privileges granted by the emperors of Trebizond. The three monasteries were allowed 'τὴν ἄμεσον ἐκκλησιαστικὴν ἐπίβλεψιν' (direct church supervision) of the 'eleftheric' villages around them. He dates the firm shape of this system to 'the last two centuries', through patriarchal sigillia, and rejects its derivation from the imperial chrysobulls, which in his view granted only the emperors' own villages and paroikoi (whose remnants are the mortitai and imiseiastai on monastery land). In 1564 the metropolitan still acted as bishop of the villages around Vazelon; on 8 November 1707 Metropolitan Paisios ceded to Vazelon the Matsouka villages Chortokopin, Chavas and Spelia 'καὶ ὅσα ρωμόχωρα εἶναι ἐξ αὐτῶν καὶ ἄνω' (and all Rum villages from there upwards).", "people": ["Selim I", "Paisios (metropolitan of Trebizond)"], "sources_primary": ["Patriarchal sigillia and Paisios' act of 8 November 1707, as cited by Chrysanthos", "Soumela tradition on Selim I"], "sources_modern": ["Chrysanthos 1933, Η Εκκλησία Τραπεζούντος (Αρχείον Πόντου 4–5), p.477; pp.683–687"], "confidence": "medium", "disputed": "Selim I's confirmation rests on monastic tradition. Chrysanthos' late dating of the exarchates is set against the view that they descend from imperial grants.", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Soumela monastery", "lat": 40.69, "lon": 39.6583, "role": "exarchal monastery"}, {"name": "Vazelon monastery", "lat": 40.7667, "lon": 39.5333, "role": "exarchal monastery; grant of 1707"}, {"name": "Peristereota monastery (Kuştul)", "lat": 40.7911, "lon": 39.72, "role": "exarchal monastery"}, {"name": "Matsouka (Maçka)", "lat": 40.8111, "lon": 39.6132, "role": "Matsouka villages ceded to Vazelon"}]}, {"id": "H5-007", "date": "1514", "date_text": "Traditionally 920/1514; the tomb beside it carries an inscription of 911/1505", "precision": "year", "title": "The Gülbahar Hatun (Hatuniye) mosque", "summary": "Selim I is generally held to have built the mosque complex (mosque, imaret, medrese, library and tomb) west of the walled city for his mother, Gülbahar Hatun; her türbe stands beside it. The mosque has no dated inscription; the tomb's kitabe is dated 911/1505, and the mosque's date of 1514 rests on Evliya Çelebi and Katip Çelebi, as accepted by Halil Edhem; the endowment charter is lost. Its quarter (İmaret-i Hatuniye) first appears in the register of 1553.", "people": ["Selim I", "Ayşe Gülbahar Hatun"], "sources_primary": ["Foundation inscription"], "sources_modern": ["Lowry 2009, The Islamization & Turkification of the City of Trabzon (Trebizond), 1461–1583, pp. 88–89", "Bryer & Winfield 1985 (Trabzon chapter)", "Lowry 2009"], "confidence": "medium", "disputed": "The date 1514 is a 17th-century tradition (Evliya Çelebi, Katip Çelebi), accepted by Halil Edhem; the mosque has no dated inscription, the tomb is dated 1505, and the quarter appears only in 1553 (Lowry).", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Gülbahar Hatun mosque, Trabzon", "lat": 41.003, "lon": 39.716, "role": "site (coordinates approximate)"}]}, {"id": "H5-008", "date": "1515", "date_text": "921 H / 1515 (register TT 52)", "precision": "year", "title": "Of valley in the register of 1515: 98% Christian", "summary": "The register of 1515 lists 60 settlements in the nahiye of Of with 2,601 taxable households: 2,550 Christian (98%) and 51 Muslim (2%). This is the baseline for the later Islamization of the valley whose descendants still speak Romeyka.", "sources_primary": ["Tahrir defter TT 52 (1515), transliterated in Hasan Umur, Of Tarihi: Vesikalar ve Fermanlar (Istanbul 1951) and Of Tarihine Ek (1956)"], "sources_modern": ["Poutouridou 1997, Δελτίο ΚΜΣ 12, pp. 48-49"], "confidence": "high", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Of", "lat": 40.946, "lon": 40.268, "role": "nahiye centre"}]}, {"id": "H5-009", "date": "1523", "date_text": "C. 1520-1523", "precision": "year", "title": "Trabzon still about 85% Christian", "summary": "Sixty years after the conquest the register of ca. 1523 (TT 387) lists 179 Muslim households (plus 22 Muslim religious heads), 893 Greek Orthodox (plus 98 widows), 151 Armenian and 40 Latin: about 7,017 residents by Lowry's reckoning, of whom 14.3% Muslim and about 86% Christian. The Muslim share had fallen since ca. 1486 (19.2%) while the city grew, so the early settlement of Muslims had not turned Trabzon Muslim.", "people": ["Süleyman I"], "sources_primary": ["Tahrir defter of Trabzon, c. 1523"], "sources_modern": ["Lowry 2009, The Islamization & Turkification of the City of Trabzon (Trebizond), 1461–1583, Tables VII–VIII (ca. 1523), pp. 62, 69", "Lowry 2009, ch. III", "Bryer 1991, p. 319 (86% Christian in 1520), as cited by Topalidis", "Bryer 1983, Δελτίο ΚΜΣ 4, p. 24 ('By ca. 1523 the Muslim element had shrunk to 14% and the Christian grown to 86%', after Lowry's 1977 dissertation, pp. 209-47)"], "confidence": "high", "disputed": "Lowry dates TT 387 to ca. 1520–23; Bryer gives 1525 for the related Gökbilgin register.", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Trabzon", "lat": 41.005, "lon": 39.726, "role": "registered city"}]}, {"id": "C3-L01", "date": "1523", "date_end": "1540", "date_text": "Between ca. 1523 and 1540 (Lowry's inference)", "precision": "decade", "title": "Trabzon Christians deported to Istanbul", "summary": "The Istanbul register of 947/1540 lists five groups \"from Trabzon\": the quarter \"Aya Trabzon\" (53 households, 2 priests), the quarter of the Trabzon Lighthouse (41, 1 priest) and three communities of fishermen (138, 18 and 26): 279 Greek households, about 1,395 people, plus 18 Armenian households. Lowry links them to the sharp fall of Trabzon's Christian population between ca. 1523 and 1553 and suggests that the Pontic tradition of deportation \"at the conquest\", repeated by Fallmerayer and Finlay, remembers this later sürgün, 62 to 79 years after 1461.", "sources_primary": ["İstanbul Evkaf Defteri of 947/1540 (as read by Lowry)"], "sources_modern": ["Lowry 2009, The Islamization & Turkification of the City of Trabzon (Trebizond), 1461–1583, pp. 105–107"], "confidence": "medium", "disputed": "Lowry: \"the available information does not allow us to state categorically\" the date; it fits the decline between ca. 1523 and 1553.", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Trabzon (western suburbs)", "lat": 41.005, "lon": 39.716, "role": "quarters emptied"}, {"name": "Istanbul", "lat": 41.0082, "lon": 28.9784, "role": "destination"}]}, {"id": "H5-010", "date": "1553", "date_text": "961 H / 1553", "precision": "year", "title": "Deportations empty the Greek quarters; Of 11% Muslim", "summary": "By 1553 the city had about 6,100 residents, 13% fewer than in ca. 1523. Greek Orthodox households fell from 893 to 509, while Muslim households rose from 179 to 570 (46.7% of the population). Lowry ascribes the fall mainly to a deportation (sürgün) of Christians to Istanbul, which he dates between ca. 1523 and 1540 (see C3-L01), partly replaced by Muslim settlers; a Muslim quarter of Aya Sofya appears for the first time. In the Of valley the same year shows 67 settlements and 3,194 households, of which 2,829 Christian (about 88%) and 365 Muslim (about 11%).", "sources_primary": ["Tahrir defter of 1553 (city)", "MM 288 (Of), in Umur 1951"], "sources_modern": ["Lowry 2009, The Islamization & Turkification of the City of Trabzon (Trebizond), 1461–1583, pp. 105–107 (the 1540 Istanbul register and the sürgün), Table XI (1553), pp. 78–79", "Lowry 2009, discussion of Table XIX and caption of Graph II", "Poutouridou 1997, p. 49"], "confidence": "medium", "disputed": "Of: Poutouridou's 1553 figures (2,829 Christian / 365 Muslim households) differ from Bryer's table for 1554 (2,629 / 359; his printed total 3,088 should be 2,988). Lowry's deportation date (ca. 1523–40) is an inference from the 1540 Istanbul register, which he presents as \"in keeping with the known facts\", not as proven.", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Trabzon", "lat": 41.005, "lon": 39.726, "role": "registered city"}, {"name": "Of", "lat": 40.946, "lon": 40.268, "role": "nahiye (register MM 288)"}]}, {"id": "C3-L02", "date": "1572", "date_text": "980/1572 or a little later", "precision": "year", "title": "Hagia Sophia of Trebizond becomes a mosque", "summary": "Mehmed Âşık relates that Kurd 'Ali Bey, a notable of Trabzon, petitioned the sultan to take the church of Hagia Sophia from the Christians to serve the Muslim quarter that had grown around it; it became the Aya Sofya Câmii. By 1583 the register lists no Christian quarter of that name, and the Christians who stayed were re-registered under Eksouthe and Valadin; the converts stayed on \"to pray in what must have already been very familiar surroundings\" (Lowry).", "people": ["Kurd 'Ali Bey", "Mehmed Âşık (author)"], "sources_primary": ["Mehmed Âşık, Menâzırü'l-avâlim (as quoted by Lowry)"], "sources_modern": ["Lowry 2009, The Islamization & Turkification of the City of Trabzon (Trebizond), 1461–1583, pp. 126, 160–161"], "confidence": "medium", "disputed": "Chrysanthos' window for the conversion (1578–1609) is later than Âşık's 1572 'or a little later'; Lowry's date rests on Âşık.", "places": [{"name": "Hagia Sophia, Trabzon", "lat": 41.0045, "lon": 39.6936, "role": "church converted"}]}, {"id": "H5-011", "date": "1583", "date_text": "991 H / 1583", "precision": "year", "title": "Trabzon in 1583: a growing city with a rising Muslim share, largely through conversion", "summary": "By 1583 the city had about 10,575 residents (+73% since 1553): 1,134 Muslim households (53.6% of the population), 811 Greek Orthodox, 123 Armenian and 47 Latin. Lowry shows that conversion now drove the change: 22.57% of the Muslims of 1583 were first-generation converts (sons of \"Abdullah\"), and one in five of the Christian households expected from 1553 appears instead as Muslim (256 of 1,237); with the second generation, 44.71% of the Muslims were converts or converts' sons. He concludes that the non-Turkish element still made up about 70% of the population and was \"in all probability … still largely Greek speaking\" (his view). In the Of valley in 1583 there were 75 villages and 4,159 households: 3,168 Christian (76%) and 991 Muslim (24%).", "people": ["Murad III"], "sources_primary": ["Tahrir defter of 1583 (TKGM 43 for Of, in Umur 1951)"], "sources_modern": ["Lowry 2009, The Islamization & Turkification of the City of Trabzon (Trebizond), 1461–1583, p. 156 (22.57%; one in five), p. 162 (44.71%; Table XXX p. 163), p. 164, p. 170 (53.62%; 70.35%); Table XX (1583), pp. 122–123", "Lowry 2009, text of the Table XIX discussion and Map V caption (10,575; +73.36%), as posted on academia.edu/9464425", "Bryer 1991, p. 319 (54% Muslim in 1583), as cited by Topalidis (not reached)", "Poutouridou 1997, p. 49", "Bryer 1983, Δελτίο ΚΜΣ 4, p. 24 ('by 1583 it had turned tables to 54:46%'; 43% of the Muslims first- or second-generation converts; after Lowry's 1977 dissertation)"], "confidence": "high", "disputed": "Of: Bryer's table gives 3,115 Christian / 976 Muslim households for 1583, against Poutouridou's 3,168 / 991. Bryer 1983 (p. 24), from Lowry's dissertation of 1977, says 43% of the Muslims of 1583 were first- or second-generation converts; Lowry 2009 (p. 162) has 44.71%.", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Trabzon", "lat": 41.005, "lon": 39.726, "role": "registered city"}, {"name": "Of", "lat": 40.946, "lon": 40.268, "role": "nahiye (register TKGM 43)"}]}, {"id": "C3-011", "date": "1600", "date_end": "1700", "date_text": "Especially the 17th century", "precision": "century", "title": "Derebeys divide the coast and the city of Trebizond", "summary": "Chrysanthos writes that local lords ('Τερὲ-βέηδες'), especially in the 17th century, held Gonia near Batum, Rize, Of, Sourmena, Matsouka, Platana, Koralla (Görele), Tripolis and Kerasous. The city of Trebizond was split into three timars of three derebeys, with night watches and loopholes in the dividing walls. In civil strife between the janissaries of the Orta-hisar and the gunners of the Kule, the old palace on the acropolis burned and the Theoskepastos convent was used as a fort.", "sources_primary": ["Triantafyllidis and local tradition, as cited by Chrysanthos"], "sources_modern": ["Chrysanthos 1933, Η Εκκλησία Τραπεζούντος (Αρχείον Πόντου 4–5), pp.706–707"], "confidence": "medium", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Trapezous (Trabzon)", "lat": 41.0024, "lon": 39.7189, "role": "split into three timars"}, {"name": "Rizaion (Rize)", "lat": 41.0208, "lon": 40.5219, "role": "derebey seat"}, {"name": "Of", "lat": 40.946, "lon": 40.27, "role": "derebey seat"}, {"name": "Sourmena (Sürmene)", "lat": 40.91046, "lon": 40.11405, "role": "derebey seat"}, {"name": "Matsouka (Maçka)", "lat": 40.8111, "lon": 39.6132, "role": "derebey seat"}, {"name": "Platana (Akçaabat)", "lat": 41.0212, "lon": 39.5715, "role": "derebey seat"}, {"name": "Koralla (Görele)", "lat": 41.0308, "lon": 39.0031, "role": "derebey seat"}, {"name": "Tripolis (Tirebolu)", "lat": 41.00735, "lon": 38.81452, "role": "derebey seat"}, {"name": "Kerasous (Giresun)", "lat": 40.917, "lon": 38.3874, "role": "derebey seat"}]}, {"id": "C3-005", "date": "1607", "date_end": "1608", "date_text": "1607 or 1608 (Boudier, writing in 1609)", "precision": "year", "title": "Celali rebels raid Trebizond", "summary": "Citing the unpublished travel account of Boudier (1609, manuscript in Paris), Chrysanthos says the Celali rebels ('Γκελολί') raided Trebizond in 1607 or 1608, plundered, and cut off the noses and ears of many Christians, but could not take the walled city.", "sources_primary": ["Boudier, travel account of 1609 (Paris, Bibliothèque nationale, manuscript), as cited by Chrysanthos"], "sources_modern": ["Chrysanthos 1933, Η Εκκλησία Τραπεζούντος (Αρχείον Πόντου 4–5), pp.694–695"], "confidence": "medium", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Trapezous (Trabzon)", "lat": 41.0024, "lon": 39.7189, "role": "raided; walled city held"}]}, {"id": "C3-006", "date": "1624", "date_end": "1767", "date_text": "Archbishopric between 1624 and 1654 (Silvestros); metropolis in July 1767 (Dionysios)", "precision": "decade", "title": "Chaldia raised to archbishopric and then metropolis", "summary": "Chrysanthos gives the rise of the see of Chaldia, seated at Argyroupolis: an archbishopric between 1624 and 1654 under Silvestros, and a metropolis in July 1767 under Dionysios, a month after its settlement with Trebizond over the mines (21 June 1767).", "people": ["Silvestros of Chaldia", "Dionysios of Chaldia"], "sources_modern": ["Chrysanthos 1933, Η Εκκλησία Τραπεζούντος (Αρχείον Πόντου 4–5), p.530; p.683"], "confidence": "medium", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Argyroupolis (Gümüşhane, old town)", "lat": 40.4598, "lon": 39.4778, "role": "seat of the see of Chaldia"}]}, {"id": "H5-012", "date": "1645", "date_text": "After 1645", "precision": "decade", "title": "The bishopric of Of disappears", "summary": "A bishopric of Of disappears from the Patriarchate's lists after 1645. This fits the conversion of the valley in the later 17th century. Bryer doubted that the see ever really functioned and called it a 'phantom bishopric'. The lack of a local church structure may have made conversion easier.", "sources_primary": ["Patriarchal notitiae/lists"], "sources_modern": ["Poutouridou 1997, p. 57 and n. 46 (citing Bryer)", "Chrysanthos 1933, Η Εκκλησία Τραπεζούντος (Αρχείον Πόντου 4–5), p.530; pp.688–689; pp.707–710"], "confidence": "medium", "disputed": "Whether the see was ever real is doubtful; Bryer calls it a 'phantom bishopric'.", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Of", "lat": 40.946, "lon": 40.268, "role": "putative see"}]}, {"id": "H5-013", "date": "1647", "date_text": "1647 (Evliya Çelebi's journey)", "precision": "year", "title": "Evliya Çelebi at the Gümüşhane mines", "summary": "Evliya Çelebi counted 70 mines, working and idle, at Gümüşhane and said the inhabitants were exempt from all taxes. The miners and the villages that supplied them held privileges: exemption from extraordinary levies and freedom from the interference of local governors. These privileges drew Greek miners to Chaldia and later shaped the crypto-Christian question.", "people": ["Evliya Çelebi"], "sources_primary": ["Evliya Çelebi, Seyahatname II (Trabzon section)"], "sources_modern": ["Karabıçak 2021, Δελτίο ΚΜΣ 21, p. 127", "Bryer 1983, Δελτίο ΚΜΣ 4, p. 32 and n. 41 (mines revived on a large scale in the second half of the 17th c. by Greek archimetallourgoi; miners and charcoal-burners excused harac for their service), pp. 32-33 and n. 43 (that status as the origin of most Pontic crypto-Christians)"], "confidence": "high", "disputed": "Topalidis has the Ottoman town of Gümüşhane founded in the 1590s; the mining district is older (the Kanca/Canca area). Bryer 1983 (p. 32) has the mines first recorded as an Ottoman mint in the 16th century and revived on a large scale only in the second half of the 17th, by Greek concessionaires (archimetallourgoi), whose miners and charcoal-burners were excused harac in return for mining or charcoal service; he makes that status the origin of most Pontic crypto-Christians (pp. 32-33).", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Gümüşhane (Argyroupolis)", "lat": 40.46, "lon": 39.481, "role": "mining town"}]}, {"id": "H5-014", "date": "1650", "date_end": "1700", "date_text": "Second half of the 17th century", "precision": "decade", "title": "The main wave of conversion in Of and Tonya", "summary": "Most historians place the decisive Islamization of the Of valley and of Tonya in the second half of the 17th century. That was a time of banditry, tax abuse and the rise of local ağas and derebeys. Local tradition blames a Bishop Alexandros of Of, who is said to have converted as Iskender after a quarrel with the metropolitan of Trebizond. Other stories tell of three Greek-speaking dervishes from Maraş, and of the priest Pavlos of Okena, forced to convert after his children were killed. Christians who refused are said to have fled over the hills to Sourmena. By the 18th century the valley was a centre of Sunni learning, with many medreses and hocas. Chrysanthos (1933, pp. 530, 688-689, 707-710) places the mass conversion of Rize, Of, Sourmena and Gimora under Mehmed IV and the Köprülü viziers, with the mixed Greek and Armenian population of Hemşin and, about the same time, Tonya; he gives the Bishop Alexandros/Iskender tradition and says the converts of Sourmena and Gimora lost their Greek, while the Ophites and the people of Tonya kept it. His view that the Muslims of the diocese descend almost entirely from converts is his own opinion; Lowry's registers contradict it, showing Muslims settled in the city by the state.", "people": ["Bishop Alexandros/Iskender (tradition)", "Priest Pavlos of Okena (tradition)"], "sources_primary": ["Oral traditions recorded by 19th-century Greek writers (Triantaphyllides 1866, Parcharides 1879)", "Ottoman orders in Umur 1951"], "sources_modern": ["Poutouridou 1997, pp. 48-61", "Meeker 1971 (IJMES 2) and 2002 (A Nation of Empire)", "Bryer & Winfield 1985, p. 156", "Mackridge 1987 (BMGS 11)", "Chrysanthos 1933, Η Εκκλησία Τραπεζούντος (Αρχείον Πόντου 4–5), p.530; pp.688–689; pp.707–710", "Ioannidis 1870, pp. 134–135, 257–258 (a council of notables and clergy, undated, chose Islam under pressure from earlier converts and the Laz; part of the population emigrated to the Crimea and Moldavia; Bishop Alexander's apostasy dated 1685; the right bank converted earlier, 'probably at the conquest') [Greek]", "Bryer 1980, study XI ('The Tourkokratia in the Pontos'), pp. 45–46 (no bishopric of Of and no Iskender Pasha recorded; the origins of the Oflu 'need further investigation') [international scholar, Britain]", "Agtzidis 2005, p. 98 (repeats the Maraş-missionaries story without a source) [Greek]", "Çilingir, pp. 17, 69–70 (an undocumented 'religion or language' decree) [Turkish]"], "confidence": "medium", "disputed": "The timing and the mechanism are debated. Greek tradition speaks of a forced, sudden mass conversion under the derebeys; Bryer & Winfield see no single mass conversion, only a gradual overtaking of the Christians by the Muslims; Meeker sees Christians withdrawing upstream while a Muslim elite tied to the Ottoman state and its learned men grew; Parcharides (1879) puts the beginnings earlier than the late 17th century; Mackridge shows that the Greek of Of keeps archaic features, so that its speakers descend from local Greek-speakers, not newcomers. Chrysanthos says that the converts of Sourmena and Gimora lost their Greek. Ioannidis (1870) tells of a council of notables and clergy who chose Islam under pressure from earlier converts and the Laz, part of the people emigrating to the Crimea and Moldavia; he dates Bishop Alexander's apostasy to 1685. Bryer finds no bishopric of Of and no Iskender Pasha recorded, and says that the origins of the people of Of 'need further investigation'. Agtzidis (2005) repeats the story of the missionaries from Maraş without a source, and Çilingir gives an undocumented decree of 'religion or language'.", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Of (lower valley)", "lat": 40.946, "lon": 40.268, "role": "converted district"}, {"name": "Çaykara (Katohor), upper Of valley", "lat": 40.747, "lon": 40.24, "role": "converted district"}, {"name": "Tonya", "lat": 40.886, "lon": 39.292, "role": "converted district"}, {"name": "Sürmene (Sourmena)", "lat": 40.912, "lon": 40.113, "role": "refuge of Christians in tradition"}]}, {"id": "C3-012", "date": "1650", "date_end": "1700", "date_text": "Mid-17th century and after", "precision": "decade", "title": "Flight of the Ypsilantis and Mourouzis families and emigration to Russia and the Danube", "summary": "From Athanasios Ypsilantis' chronicle, Chrysanthos tells how the Ypsilantis and Mourouzis families fled Trebizond in the mid-17th century because the pasha demanded Triantafyllos Ypsilantis' daughter; their property was confiscated and sold to Turks. He traces their later Phanariot careers, and writes more generally that many fled 'τὰς πιέσεις καὶ τὸν βίᾳ ἐπιβαλλόμενον ἐξισλαμισμὸν' (the pressures and the forced Islamization) to Russia and Moldo-Wallachia.", "people": ["Triantafyllos Ypsilantis", "Athanasios Ypsilantis"], "sources_primary": ["Athanasios Ypsilantis, chronicle, as cited by Chrysanthos"], "sources_modern": ["Chrysanthos 1933, Η Εκκλησία Τραπεζούντος (Αρχείον Πόντου 4–5), pp.697–705; p.710"], "confidence": "medium", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Trapezous (Trabzon)", "lat": 41.0024, "lon": 39.7189, "role": "place of departure"}, {"name": "Constantinople (Istanbul)", "lat": 41.008, "lon": 28.978, "role": "Phanar"}, {"name": "Bucharest", "lat": 44.4268, "lon": 26.1025, "role": "Moldo-Wallachia"}]}, {"id": "C3-008", "date": "1659-05", "date_text": "May 1659", "precision": "month", "title": "Patriarch Paisios of Jerusalem visits Trebizond", "summary": "Chrysanthos writes that Patriarch Paisios of Jerusalem, with the deacon Dositheos (the later patriarch), came to Trebizond in May 1659 and celebrated in St Philip; the pasha tried to harass him and the kadi freed him; he went on to Soumela and to Kanin (Argyroupolis). Chrysanthos uses the visit as evidence of the pasha's cruelty in this period, when Metropolitan Philotheos (1659–1665) had to flee.", "people": ["Paisios of Jerusalem", "Dositheos of Jerusalem", "Philotheos (metropolitan of Trebizond)"], "sources_primary": ["Dositheos of Jerusalem, as cited by Chrysanthos"], "sources_modern": ["Chrysanthos 1933, Η Εκκλησία Τραπεζούντος (Αρχείον Πόντου 4–5), pp.696–697; pp.705–706"], "confidence": "medium", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Trapezous (Trabzon)", "lat": 41.0024, "lon": 39.7189, "role": "liturgy at St Philip"}, {"name": "Soumela monastery", "lat": 40.69, "lon": 39.6583, "role": "visited"}, {"name": "Argyroupolis (Gümüşhane, old town)", "lat": 40.4598, "lon": 39.4778, "role": "Kanin, visited"}]}, {"id": "C3-009", "date": "1665", "date_end": "1674", "date_text": "1665 (verse narrative in a Vazelon codex) or 1674 (Dositheos); lawsuits until 1674", "precision": "decade", "title": "St Philip, the cathedral, seized and made a mosque", "summary": "Chrysanthos tells how the Turks of the city, led by the mufti and backed by the kızlar ağası, seized the cathedral of St Philip on a Friday. A verse narrative in a Vazelon codex dates it to 1665, Dositheos to 1674, and there were lawsuits until 1674. The metropolis then moved to the monastery of St Gregory of Nyssa, whose church served as the cathedral. Hagia Sophia, a mosque at times since the 16th century, became a mosque for good between 1660 and 1665.", "sources_primary": ["Verse narrative of the capture of the metropolis (Vazelon codex; Papadopoulos-Kerameus, Fontes), as cited by Chrysanthos", "Dositheos of Jerusalem"], "sources_modern": ["Chrysanthos 1933, Η Εκκλησία Τραπεζούντος (Αρχείον Πόντου 4–5), pp.695–696; pp.711–713"], "confidence": "medium", "disputed": "The two dates (1665 and 1674) come from different sources; Chrysanthos does not choose.", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Trapezous (Trabzon)", "lat": 41.0024, "lon": 39.7189, "role": "St Philip seized; cathedral moved to St Gregory of Nyssa"}]}, {"id": "C3-010", "date": "1681-04-06", "date_text": "6 April 1681 (Dositheos' arrival at Trebizond)", "precision": "day", "title": "Dositheos of Jerusalem describes a Pontus nearly empty of Christians", "summary": "Chrysanthos prints Dositheos' account of 1681: from Sourmena to Guria and the Chorokh 'πρὸ ἑβδομήκοντα ἐτῶν ἤρχισαν οἱ Λαζοὶ σαρακηνίζειν, καὶ οὐκ ἔμεινεν οὐδὲ μία ἐκκλησία, ἢ ἱερεύς' (seventy years ago the Laz began to turn Muslim, and not one church or priest remained), only some women. Amastris had churches but no Christians; Tieion, Kromna and Kotyora were deserted; Oinoe had five Orthodox villages of about 80 houses; Amisos had not one Christian, with a single Christian village nearby; Neocaesarea had none, Amaseia 'ὀλιγοστοί' (a very few); some remained at Kerasous and Tripolis.", "people": ["Dositheos of Jerusalem"], "sources_primary": ["Dositheos of Jerusalem, report of 1681, as quoted by Chrysanthos"], "sources_modern": ["Chrysanthos 1933, Η Εκκλησία Τραπεζούντος (Αρχείον Πόντου 4–5), p.708; pp.710–711"], "confidence": "medium", "disputed": "A traveller's impression; he gives numbers only for Oinoe.", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Sourmena (Sürmene)", "lat": 40.91046, "lon": 40.11405, "role": "western limit of the Laz conversions"}, {"name": "Amastris (Amasra)", "lat": 41.746, "lon": 32.386, "role": "churches without Christians"}, {"name": "Kotyora (Ordu)", "lat": 40.97782, "lon": 37.89047, "role": "deserted"}, {"name": "Oinoe (Ünye)", "lat": 41.140493, "lon": 37.28851, "role": "five Orthodox villages, c. 80 houses"}, {"name": "Amisos (Samsun)", "lat": 41.279722, "lon": 36.335833, "role": "no Christians"}, {"name": "Neocaesarea (Niksar)", "lat": 40.591667, "lon": 36.951667, "role": "no Christians"}, {"name": "Amaseia (Amasya)", "lat": 40.65, "lon": 35.8331, "role": "very few Christians"}, {"name": "Kerasous (Giresun)", "lat": 40.917, "lon": 38.3874, "role": "some Christians"}, {"name": "Tripolis (Tirebolu)", "lat": 41.00735, "lon": 38.81452, "role": "some Christians"}]}, {"id": "H5-015", "date": "1683", "date_end": "1689", "date_text": "1683-1689 (scholarch: Chrysanthos); he left Constantinople in 1682 and was in Trebizond by February 1683; often given as 'founded 1682'", "precision": "year", "title": "Sevastos Kyminitis heads the Phrontisterion of Trapezous (1683-1689)", "summary": "Sevastos Kyminitis, born at Kymina (Kotsi, Hotz) near Trebizond, taught at the Patriarchal School in Constantinople from 1671 and headed Manolakis Manos' school in the Phanar from 1676 until 1682, when a students' revolt made him resign. Invited home by the Trapezuntines (he turned down a call to Moscow), and partly to counter Catholic monks who had begun teaching young Trapezuntines free of charge about 1682, he took over the direction of the Phrontisterion in 1683 and was its scholarch until 1689; a letter addressed to him at Trebizond in February 1683 shows he had arrived by then. Chrysanthos stresses that teaching had gone on there before 1683: Kyminitis organised and systematised the school rather than founding it. Merchant families such as the Velissarides and Kallivazis are said to have supported it (Salvanou). He went to Bucharest about 1689/90, headed the Princely Academy under Constantin Brâncoveanu, and died there on 6 September 1702. The school ran until November 1921 and trained the region's teachers and clergy.", "people": ["Sevastos Kyminitis", "Constantin Brâncoveanu"], "sources_primary": ["Kyminitis' letter of 1682 on his resignation, and the letter of Ephraim the hieromonk to him at Trebizond dated 1683, Anthesterion (February), both quoted by Chrysanthos 1933, pp. 727-728", "Kyminitis' own works and letters"], "sources_modern": ["Bryer & Winfield 2006 (The Post-… Monuments of Pontos), p. xxxiii", "Salvanou, Encyclopaedia of the Hellenic World, Asia Minor, 'Phrontisterion of Trapezous'", "Pavlidis, Το Φροντιστήριο Τραπεζούντας (1900-1914), Αρχείον Πόντου suppl. 24", "Chrysanthos 1933, Η Εκκλησία Τραπεζούντος (Αρχείον Πόντου 4–5), p.720; pp.726–729 (1683; 1676-1682; scholarch 1683-1689)", "Bryer 1980, study XII ('The Pontic Revival and the New Greece', 1976), p. 185 ('a school founded by Sebastos Kyminites in 1682', citing Kyriakides 1897 and Oikonomides 1936-37)", "Ioannidis 1870, p. 151 and p. 226", "Kyriakidis 1898, p. 187", "Agtzidis, Έλληνες του Πόντου ('founded in 1682')"], "confidence": "high", "disputed": "Both 1682 and 1683 are right, for different things: in 1682 he resigned in Constantinople and came home, and in 1683, the year Chrysanthos gives, he took over the school; a letter of February 1683 already finds him at Trebizond. 'Founded in 1682' (Bryer, after Kyriakides and Oikonomides; Agtzidis; Salvanou) runs the two together and makes him the founder of a school that Chrysanthos and Ioannidis (who says it was called the Phrontisterion 'from time immemorial') say existed before him. Some accounts have the school first sit at Soumela; Kyriakidis (1898) says that its first house was the monastery's metochion in the city, and Bryer that it was long run by the monastery of Peristereota.", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Trabzon (Phrontisterion)", "lat": 41.005, "lon": 39.726, "role": "school"}, {"name": "Constantinople, Patriarchal Academy", "lat": 41.029, "lon": 28.952, "role": "Kyminitis' earlier post"}, {"name": "Bucharest, Princely Academy", "lat": 44.427, "lon": 26.103, "role": "Kyminitis' later post"}]}, {"id": "H5-016", "date": "1695", "date_text": "1106 H / 1694-95", "precision": "year", "title": "The Porte asks Of for 1,000 soldiers", "summary": "An imperial order demanded 1,000 soldiers from Of. It implies that the valley had a large Muslim (liable to serve) population by the 1690s, a terminus for the main wave of conversion.", "people": ["Mustafa II"], "sources_primary": ["Ferman in Umur 1951"], "sources_modern": ["Poutouridou 1997, p. 56 n. 42"], "confidence": "medium", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Of", "lat": 40.946, "lon": 40.268, "role": "levy"}]}, {"id": "C3-014", "date": "1698", "date_end": "1774", "date_text": "Annexed in 1698 at Nektarios' request; fully united by a sigillion of July 1774", "precision": "year", "title": "Kerasous exarchate annexed to the metropolis of Trebizond", "summary": "Chrysanthos writes that the exarchate of Kerasous, once a bishopric of Neocaesarea, raised to a metropolis in the second half of the 11th century and reduced to an exarchate in the mid-17th, was annexed to Trebizond in 1698 at Metropolitan Nektarios' request, in return for an annual payment. A sigillion of Patriarch Samuel Hantzeris of July 1774 united Kerasous, Tripolis and Koralla fully with Trebizond.", "people": ["Nektarios (metropolitan of Trebizond)", "Samuel Hantzeris"], "sources_primary": ["Patriarchal acts of 1698 and July 1774, as cited by Chrysanthos"], "sources_modern": ["Chrysanthos 1933, Η Εκκλησία Τραπεζούντος (Αρχείον Πόντου 4–5), p.580; p.609; p.687"], "confidence": "medium", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Kerasous (Giresun)", "lat": 40.917, "lon": 38.3874, "role": "exarchate annexed"}, {"name": "Tripolis (Tirebolu)", "lat": 41.00735, "lon": 38.81452, "role": "united with Trebizond 1774"}, {"name": "Koralla (Görele)", "lat": 41.0308, "lon": 39.0031, "role": "united with Trebizond 1774"}, {"name": "Trapezous (Trabzon)", "lat": 41.0024, "lon": 39.7189, "role": "metropolis"}]}, {"id": "C3-015", "date": "1706", "date_end": "1767", "date_text": "Nektarios at Argyroupolis 1706; Paisios' quarrel 1709–1717; settlement of 21 June 1767", "precision": "decade", "title": "The mines and the miners' clergy: disputes between Trebizond and Chaldia", "summary": "In 1706 Nektarios visited Argyroupolis 'ἐπὶ τῆς ἀκμῆς τῶν μεταλλείων' (at the height of the mines) to collect alms. Paisios quarrelled with Archbishop Philotheos of Chaldia, who relied on 'τοὺς προκρίτους ἀρχιμεταλλουργοὺς τῆς Ἀργυροπόλεως' (the leading mine-masters of Argyroupolis); in 1711 Paisios collected alms from the rich miners, and on 1 July 1717 he came with a royal order, exiled Philotheos and ran Chaldia until a successor was elected. On 21 June 1767, before Patriarch Samuel Hantzeris, Trebizond and Chaldia settled their dispute over Kerasous, Tripolis and Koralla 'μετὰ τῶν ἐν αὐτοῖς μαδενίων' (with their mines): Trebizond kept the parish rights in the mines and the appointment of their priests, while Chaldia could collect dues from its own diocesans working there. Chrysanthos calls the arrangement uncanonical, imposed by the Chaldian mine-masters, 'powerful at the Porte'.", "people": ["Nektarios", "Paisios (metropolitan of Trebizond)", "Philotheos of Chaldia", "Dorotheos III", "Dionysios of Chaldia", "Samuel Hantzeris"], "sources_primary": ["Codex of the Argyroupolis cathedral; notes dated 1711 and 1 July 1717; patriarchal act of 21 June 1767, as cited by Chrysanthos"], "sources_modern": ["Chrysanthos 1933, Η Εκκλησία Τραπεζούντος (Αρχείον Πόντου 4–5), p.584; pp.591–592; pp.604–606", "Bryer 1983, Δελτίο ΚΜΣ 4, p. 33 n. 43"], "confidence": "medium", "disputed": "Bryer 1983 (p. 33 n. 43) adds that the archbishops of Chaldia, often of the Phytianos family which also supplied the archimetallourgoi of Argyroupolis, extended their exarchate to 'All Metal-Bearing Lands', including the alum mines around Tzanchraki, and that Chaldia claimed a 'corridor' to the sea at Bulancak just before the First World War.", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Argyroupolis (Gümüşhane, old town)", "lat": 40.4598, "lon": 39.4778, "role": "mines; mine-masters"}, {"name": "Kerasous (Giresun)", "lat": 40.917, "lon": 38.3874, "role": "mines in dispute"}, {"name": "Tripolis (Tirebolu)", "lat": 41.00735, "lon": 38.81452, "role": "mines in dispute"}, {"name": "Koralla (Görele)", "lat": 41.0308, "lon": 39.0031, "role": "mines in dispute"}, {"name": "Trapezous (Trabzon)", "lat": 41.0024, "lon": 39.7189, "role": "metropolis"}, {"name": "Constantinople (Istanbul)", "lat": 41.008, "lon": 28.978, "role": "Patriarchate; settlement of 1767"}]}, {"id": "H5-017", "date": "1709", "date_text": "1709", "precision": "year", "title": "Of villages deported to 'Anakra' in Georgia", "summary": "After resisting the tax collectors, seven villages and two clans of Of were ordered moved to 'Anakra' in Georgia. It shows that the Porte was struggling to control the valley's armed Muslim clans.", "sources_primary": ["Ferman in Umur 1951"], "sources_modern": ["Poutouridou 1997, p. 60"], "confidence": "medium", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Of", "lat": 40.946, "lon": 40.268, "role": "origin"}, {"name": "Anaklia (identification probable)", "lat": 42.393, "lon": 41.573, "role": "destination"}]}, {"id": "H5-018", "date": "1728", "date_text": "1140 H / 1727-28", "precision": "year", "title": "The Porte tells officials to stop oppressing the Gümüşhane miners", "summary": "An imperial order told local officials not to oppress the miners of Gümüşhane, a sign that ağas, officials and bandits were already breaking the miners' privileges. Karabıçak blames the long decline on lack of technology and capital and on these breaches. Fuel shortage and flooding of deep workings are the usual further explanations.", "sources_primary": ["Ottoman order of 1727/28 (cited by Karabıçak)"], "sources_modern": ["Karabıçak 2021, p. 127", "Bryer 1983, Δελτίο ΚΜΣ 4, p. 32"], "confidence": "medium", "disputed": "On the causes of the decline: Karabıçak stresses technology and capital; Bryer (1983) calls it 'a moot question whether the ore was exhausted before the fuel needed to smelt it', and has the mines in their 'last gasp' by 1829 and closed soon after. Flooding is reported in the provincial yearbook of 1870, cited by Karabıçak (H5-041).", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Gümüşhane", "lat": 40.46, "lon": 39.481, "role": "mining town"}]}, {"id": "H5-019", "date": "1730", "date_text": "C. 1730 (tradition)", "precision": "decade", "title": "Santa's tradition of a conversion to save a pasture", "summary": "A Santa story tells that villagers became nominal Muslims about 1730 to win a pasture dispute. Tzedopoulos thinks the story may be invented, but it shows how crypto-Christianity was explained locally. Before 1856 about half of Santa's people were said to be crypto-Christians.", "sources_primary": ["Local tradition"], "sources_modern": ["Tzedopoulos 2009, p. 175 n. 34", "Bryer 1983, Δελτίο ΚΜΣ 4, p. 39 (Santa's eight hamlets in the Kromlides' list of 1857: 615 houses, 285 Kurumli, 330 Greek, no Muslims), p. 42 n. 63 and 65; Document 2 (G. A. Stevens, 10 Nov 1857), pp. 42-43 (the pasture dispute)"], "confidence": "low", "disputed": "This is legend, possibly invented; Bryer (1983) does not mention it. The Kromlides' own list of 1857 gives Santa's eight hamlets 615 houses, 285 of them crypto-Christian (46 percent), 330 Greek and none Muslim, which bears out 'about half'. A British consular despatch of 10 November 1857 describes Santa's grazing ground, 'held by them by Royal Firman, since time immemorial', and its seizure by Muslims of Meşehor in June 1857 (Bryer 1983); Cheimonides (1902) describes the dispute.", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Santa (Dumanlı)", "lat": 40.68, "lon": 39.784, "role": "village group"}]}, {"id": "H5-020", "date": "1763", "date_text": "1763", "precision": "year", "title": "Erekle II brings Gümüşhane miners to Akhtala and Alaverdi", "summary": "King Erekle II of Kartli-Kakheti brought Greek miners from the Gümüşhane district to work the silver, lead and copper mines of Akhtala and Alaverdi in Lori. The usual figure, 800 households, goes back to a Greek church article of 1910. They founded the oldest Pontic communities of the southern Caucasus, and the Fytiana metropolitans later held Akhtala as a Georgian see. In the same year the Ottomans themselves called for miners from Gümüşhane for Ergani and Keban.", "people": ["Erekle II (Heraclius II)"], "sources_primary": ["Georgian royal documents"], "sources_modern": ["Panaretos (Vazeliotis), 'Οι Έλληνες του Καυκάσου', Εκκλησιαστική Αλήθεια 1910 (800 households) (as cited on Wikipedia)", "Karabıçak 2021, p. 122 (Fytiana and Akhtala), p. 128 n. 32 (Ergani and Keban, 1763)"], "confidence": "medium", "disputed": "Other accounts say 'thousands', and some give 800 workers, not households.", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Gümüşhane", "lat": 40.46, "lon": 39.481, "role": "origin"}, {"name": "Akhtala", "lat": 41.151, "lon": 44.764, "role": "destination (silver-lead mines)"}, {"name": "Alaverdi", "lat": 41.097, "lon": 44.656, "role": "destination (copper)"}, {"name": "Tbilisi", "lat": 41.716, "lon": 44.783, "role": "Erekle II's capital"}]}, {"id": "C3-016", "date": "1764-08-06", "date_text": "6 August 1764 (Transfiguration), after a 74-day war", "precision": "day", "title": "Kerasous taken and its derebey drowned", "summary": "From a note in a manuscript nomokanon of the Kerasous community, Chrysanthos records that on 6 August 1764 Kerasous 'ἠχμαλωτίσθη … ὑπὸ τοῦ Χατζίπεγιν' (was taken by Hacı Bey) after a war of 74 days ('ἡμέρας οδ´'); its derebey Tistaroglou was drowned and the town and its church were ravaged.", "people": ["Hacı Bey", "Tistaroglou"], "sources_primary": ["Note in a manuscript nomokanon of the Kerasous community, as quoted by Chrysanthos"], "sources_modern": ["Chrysanthos 1933, Η Εκκλησία Τραπεζούντος (Αρχείον Πόντου 4–5), p.713"], "confidence": "medium", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Kerasous (Giresun)", "lat": 40.917, "lon": 38.3874, "role": "town taken and ravaged"}]}, {"id": "H5-021", "date": "1770", "date_end": "1808", "date_text": "C. 1770-1808", "precision": "decade", "title": "The Caniklizade derebeys of Samsun", "summary": "The Caniklizade family ruled Canik (Samsun) as quasi-independent ayan. Its leaders were Hacı Ali Pasha (born 1720/21, died 1785 at Erzurum), Battal Hüseyin Pasha, defeated and taken prisoner by the Russians near Anapa in 1790, and Tayyar Mahmud Pasha, executed in 1808 under Mahmud II. In the eastern Pontus, where Bryer counts fourteen derebey families, each largely confined to one valley, the Tuzcuoğulları of Rize and Of played the same game; the Hazinedarzade, originally from Adjara, held the pashalik of Trebizond (with one break) from 1811 to 1844 and put down the derebeys in 1812–1840. Their feuds made the countryside insecure and pushed highland Christians to seek protection, convert or move.", "people": ["Canikli Hacı Ali Pasha", "Battal Hüseyin Pasha", "Tayyar Mahmud Pasha", "Hazinedarzade Süleyman Pasha"], "sources_primary": ["Ottoman archives"], "sources_modern": ["Bryer 1969, 'The last Laz risings and the downfall of the Pontic derebeys, 1812-1840', Bedi Kartlisa 26", "BlackSea port-cities project, 'Hazinedarzade' (Karagöz)", "Kure Ansiklopedisi, 'Canikli Hacı Ali Paşa ailesi' (kureansiklopedi.com/tr/detay/canikli-haci-ali-pasa-ailesi; bibliography incl. Uzunçarşılı, Shaw, Özkaya, Lewis in EI²) [Turkish]", "Bryer 1980, XI ('The Tourkokratia in the Pontos', 1970) (fourteen Pontic derebey families; 'a major dynasty in Canik'); XI pp. 50–51; XII ('The Pontic revival and the new Greece', 1976) n. 4 (the 'originally Adjarian Hazinedar dynasty of Canik held the pashalik with one break between 1811 and 1844')"], "confidence": "medium", "disputed": "The dates come from a Turkish reference work and from Bryer.", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Samsun (Amisos)", "lat": 41.287, "lon": 36.33, "role": "seat of the Caniklizade"}, {"name": "Trabzon", "lat": 41.005, "lon": 39.726, "role": "seat of the Hazinedarzade"}, {"name": "Rize", "lat": 41.025, "lon": 40.517, "role": "seat of the Tuzcuoğulları"}]}, {"id": "H5-022", "date": "1791", "date_text": "1789-1791", "precision": "year", "title": "Gümüşhane miners scattered across Anatolia's mines", "summary": "A 1789 order told the mine intendant (emin) to recall miners who had left for other trades. A 1791 order dealt with Gümüşhane miners working at Ergani, Keban and Bozkır and kept them under the metropolitan of Chaldia for church dues and cizye. The Chaldiot mining diaspora also reached Akdağ Madeni in Yozgat, where the Stavriote crypto-Christians later lived.", "sources_primary": ["Ottoman orders of 1789 and 1791 (cited by Karabıçak)"], "sources_modern": ["Karabıçak 2021, pp. 128-129", "Tzedopoulos 2009, p. 182 (Stavriotes at Akdağ)", "Bryer 1983, Δελτίο ΚΜΣ 4, p. 32 (Kurumlis to 'new mines at Ak Dag in 1832'), p. 33 n. 43 (Ak Dag, Ala Dag, Sin Maden, Bulgur Maden, Bulgur Dag)"], "confidence": "medium", "disputed": "Bryer (1983) dates the move to 'new mines at Ak Dag' to 1832, after the Gümüşhane mines had closed, and says that the crypto-Christian grievances after 1857 came largely from mining communities settled by Kromlides after that date, at Ak Dag, Ala Dag, Sin Maden, Bulgur Maden and Bulgur Dag, in the provinces of Konya, Ankara and Sivas. He does not say which Ak Dag; this atlas takes it to be Akdağ Madeni in Yozgat.", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Gümüşhane", "lat": 40.46, "lon": 39.481, "role": "origin"}, {"name": "Ergani Madeni (Maden)", "lat": 38.392, "lon": 39.675, "role": "copper mine"}, {"name": "Keban", "lat": 38.795, "lon": 38.737, "role": "silver-lead mine"}, {"name": "Akdağ Madeni", "lat": 39.66, "lon": 35.884, "role": "silver-lead mine; Stavriote settlement"}]}], "turkish": [], "synthesis": "## After the conquest\n\n<figure class=\"nmap\"><a href=\"maps/ch07_ottoman.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img src=\"maps/ch07_ottoman.jpg\" width=\"2000\" height=\"1120\" loading=\"lazy\" alt=\"Relief map of the eastern Pontus and Anatolia showing Selim's campaigns from Trabzon, the deportation of Christian households to Istanbul, and the miners of Gümüşhane sent to other mines.\"></a><figcaption>After the conquest: Selim's campaigns from Trabzon in 1505–1510, the Greek households sent to Istanbul about 1523–40, the miners of Gümüşhane sent to other mines, and the derebey families of Samsun, Rize and Of. Tap the map to enlarge it. Relief from the AWS Terrain Tiles; coast and rivers from Natural Earth.</figcaption></figure>\n\nTrebizond surrendered to Mehmed II in the summer of 1461. The court of David Komnenos and the leading families were shipped to Constantinople, a garrison of 400 janissaries took the citadel, and Kasım Pasha was named governor. Kritoboulos says that 1,500 children were taken as the sultan's slaves; the continuator of Chalkokondyles says that the townspeople were split three ways, some resettled in the capital, some enslaved and some sent out to the countryside, and that 800 boys were taken for the janissaries. Neither figure is a count. Bryer found signs that groups in the countryside resisted for up to ten years, and Greek folk tradition remembers castles in the hills that held out after the city fell. The Chrysokephalos became a mosque at once; St Eugenios followed by about 1523, and Hagia Sophia in 1572 or a little later.\n\nIn Matzouka (Maçka), the valley of Soumela, Vazelon and Peristera, the Ottomans kept Christian timar-holders from the old Trapezuntine landowners for decades and confirmed the monasteries' estates by firman. The three monasteries kept their lands and villages through the whole Ottoman period, and later ranked as patriarchal exarchates with direct charge of the \"free\" villages around them (Lowry 1986). The tradition that Selim I or Mehmed II endowed Soumela in person is monastic legend.\n\nThe church survived on a smaller scale. Chrysanthos writes that the eleven bishoprics that had flourished under the empire died out, and that only two were formed from their remnants: Of, and Kanin, that is Chaldia and Cheriana. The metropolitan was recognised as head of the Orthodox community, with church courts of his own. He held office at the sultan's pleasure: in 1472 Metropolitan Pankratios was driven from his throne \"by the sovereign's will\" and made to resign in writing, probably, Chrysanthos thought, because of Uzun Hasan's campaign of that year to restore Trebizond.\n\n## The registers: how Trabzon became Muslim\n\nThe Ottoman tax registers, the *tahrir defters*, make it possible to follow the city's population household by household. Heath Lowry's study of them (a thesis of 1977, published in Turkish in 1981 and in English in 2009) is the foundation of what follows.\n\nAbout 1486 the city was, in Lowry's word, \"predominately Christian\". Its Muslims were mainly the garrison, officials and settlers moved in by *sürgün*, forced resettlement: 202 households from nineteen towns of central Anatolia, 78 percent of the permanent Muslim population. Lowry's tables give 258 Muslim, 749 Greek Orthodox, 130 Armenian and 34 Latin households, about 6,700 people, of whom 19 percent were Muslim. About 1523 the register lists 179 Muslim households, 893 Greek Orthodox (with 98 widows), 151 Armenian and 40 Latin: about 7,000 people, 14 percent Muslim. The Muslim share had *fallen* since 1486.\n\nThen the city changed quickly. By 1553 it had about 6,100 people, and the Greek Orthodox households had fallen from 893 to 509 while the Muslim households had risen from 179 to 570, 47 percent of the population. Lowry blames a deportation of Christians to Istanbul, which he dates between about 1523 and 1540: the Istanbul register of 1540 lists 279 Greek households \"from Trabzon\", among them three communities of fishermen and a quarter called \"Aya Trabzon\". He suggests that the Pontic tradition of a deportation \"at the conquest\" remembers this later one, sixty or seventy years after 1461. By 1583 the city had grown to about 10,600 people, with 1,134 Muslim households, 811 Greek Orthodox, 123 Armenian and 47 Latin; Muslims were 54 percent. Many of them carry the convert's patronymic \"bin Abdullah\": 23 percent of the Muslims of 1583 were converts themselves, and counting their sons 45 percent were converts or the sons of converts. One in five of the Christian households expected from 1553 appears instead as Muslim.\n\nLowry's conclusion is that the first Muslims came by forced settlement, but that the city stayed 80–86 percent Christian until the 1520s; the change came from the deportation of Christians to Istanbul and then from conversion, all within about a century. The registers support neither a sudden forced conversion nor a story of settlement alone.\n\n## Selim's Trabzon\n\nPrince Selim, the future Selim I, governed the province of Trabzon from 1487 (some say 1481) to 1510, and his son Süleyman, the future Süleyman the Magnificent, was born there on 6 November 1494 (or 27 April 1495). From Trabzon Selim drove back a Safavid raid in 1505, attacked Erzincan and Bayburt in 1507, invaded western Georgia in 1508 and beat another Safavid force in 1510. These campaigns tied the Pontic hinterland firmly to the Ottomans. The mosque of Gülbahar Hatun, built for his mother, who died in the city, is traditionally dated 1514; the tomb beside it bears an inscription of 1505.\n\n## Disorder in the 17th century\n\nThe 17th century was hard. The Celali rebels raided Trebizond in 1607 or 1608, plundered and mutilated many Christians, but could not take the walled city. Local lords, the *derebeys*, held the valleys from Rize to Kerasous, and Chrysanthos says that the city itself was split among three of them, with walls and loopholes between their quarters. The Ypsilantis and Mourouzis families, later great Phanariot houses, fled Trebizond in the mid-17th century after the pasha demanded a daughter of Triantafyllos Ypsilantis, and many others left for Russia and the Danubian principalities. The cathedral of St Philip was seized and made a mosque in 1665 (Dositheos says 1674), and the metropolis moved to the monastery of St Gregory of Nyssa. In 1681 Dositheos, patriarch of Jerusalem, described a coast nearly empty of Christians: from Sourmena to Guria \"not one church or priest\" remained, Amisos and Neocaesarea had no Christians, and only Kerasous and Tripolis kept some.\n\n## The conversions in the valleys: Of, Sürmene and Tonya\n\n<figure class=\"nmap\"><a href=\"maps/ch07_valleys.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img src=\"maps/ch07_valleys.jpg\" width=\"2000\" height=\"1000\" loading=\"lazy\" alt=\"Relief map of the coast from Trabzon to Hopa showing where Muslims still speak Pontic Greek, the Laz coast, the crypto-Christian villages of Santa, Stavri and Kromni, and the monasteries of Matzouka.\"></a><figcaption>The valleys east and south of Trabzon: where Muslims still speak Pontic Greek (Romeyka), the Laz coast, the crypto-Christian villages, and the monasteries of Matzouka. The areas are drawn schematically. Tap the map to enlarge it. Relief from the AWS Terrain Tiles; coast and rivers from Natural Earth.</figcaption></figure>\n\nThe registers, as transliterated by Hasan Umur (1951) and analysed by Poutouridou (1997), give the clearest numbers for the valley of Of.\n\n| Year | Settlements | Households | Christian | Muslim |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 1515 | 60 | 2,601 | 2,550 (98%) | 51 (2%) |\n| 1553 | 67 | 3,194 | 2,829 (88%) | 365 (11%) |\n| 1583 | 75 | 4,159 | 3,168 (76%) | 991 (24%) |\n\nOf was still three-quarters Christian 120 years after the conquest. The turning point came in the 17th century, after the registers stop. A bishopric of Of disappears from the Patriarchate's lists after 1645, though Bryer doubted it had ever functioned; in 1694–95 the Porte demanded 1,000 soldiers from Of, which implies a large Muslim population; and in 1709 seven rebellious villages and two clans were ordered deported to \"Anakra\" in Georgia. By 1879 Parcharides counted 10,000–12,000 families in Of, of whom 8,000–10,000 spoke Greek, but only 192 were Christian. Tonya is usually dated to the late 17th century as well, on weaker evidence.\n\nHow it happened is debated. The Greek accounts disagree among themselves: Chrysanthos (1933) has one forced wave of conversion under Mehmed IV, while Ioannidis (1870) has a council of notables and clergy choosing Islam under pressure from earlier converts and the Laz, with part of the people emigrating to the Crimea and Moldavia. Both tell of Bishop Alexandros of Of, said to have become a Muslim, Iskender, after quarrelling with the metropolitan of Trebizond; Bryer found no record of such a bishop. Bryer & Winfield (1985) see no single mass conversion, only a gradual overtaking of the Christians by the Muslims. Meeker (1971, 2002) sees Christians withdrawing upstream while a local Muslim elite grew round the Ottoman state and its learned men, which is what made Of famous for its *hocas*. Mackridge (1987) shows that the Greek of Of keeps archaic features, such as the infinitive, so its speakers descend from the old Greek-speaking population, not from newcomers. Poutouridou sees conversion as partly a defence against violence and tax abuse, going on for centuries.\n\nRomeyka, the Pontic Greek of Of, is still spoken by Muslims in Çaykara, with Uzungöl, in Dernekpazarı, eastern Sürmene and Köprübaşı, a few villages of Of, and İkizdere in Rize, and there are scattered Pontic-speaking villages in Tonya and Maçka. Estimates of the number of speakers range from as few as 5,000, in press reports of Sitaridou's work, to considerably more (Özkan 2013). These people were never part of the exchange of 1923, which was defined by religion.\n\n## The Laz and the Hemshin\n\nThe Laz are speakers of a Kartvelian language on the coast from Rize to Hopa: Pazar (the old Athina), Ardeşen, Fındıklı, Arhavi and Hopa. Dositheos dates their conversion to Islam to the early 17th century. To Greeks, \"Laz\" often meant any Muslim of the eastern coast, the Greek-speakers of Of included. The Hemshin are Armenian in origin; the western Hemshinli of Rize became Muslims mainly in the late 17th and 18th centuries and now speak Turkish, while those of Hopa still speak Homshetsma (Simonian 2007).\n\n## The crypto-Christians\n\nIn the mining highlands between Trebizond and Gümüşhane lived families who were Muslim on the register and Christian at home. Greeks called them *klostoi*, \"turned\", *Kromlides*, after Kromni (Kurum), or *Stavriotes*, after Stavri (İstavri). Most writers date their beginning to the 17th-century wave of conversion. Bryer linked their persistence to the mines: miners held tax and service privileges, and passing as Muslims protected them. Tzedopoulos (2009) accepts that the mines kept such double identities alive, but not that they created them, and notes that some Kromlides may have been Muslims who had become Greek in speech rather than descendants of converts. No Western traveller mentions them before 1833. A story from Santa tells that the villagers became nominal Muslims about 1730 to win a pasture dispute; Tzedopoulos thinks it may be invented, but it shows how the people themselves explained their double faith. Their coming out after 1856 belongs to the next chapter.\n\n## The silver of Chaldia\n\nGümüşhane, the Greek Argyroupolis, was the Ottoman Empire's main silver and lead field in the east. Bryer (1983) has the mines revived on a large scale in the second half of the 17th century by Greek concessionaires, the *archimetallourgoi*, whose miners and charcoal-burners were excused the poll tax in return for their work; in 1647 Evliya Çelebi counted 70 mines and found the inhabitants exempt from all taxes. Greek mining families such as the Fytianoi held concessions, and the church followed the mines: Chaldia, seated at Argyroupolis, became an archbishopric in the 17th century and a metropolis in 1767, a month after it settled with Trebizond which of them could collect dues from the miners.\n\nThe decline is visible from the 1720s. An order of 1727–28 told officials to stop oppressing the miners, and one of 1789 told the mine intendant to recall miners who had drifted off; Karabıçak (2021) blames lack of technology and capital and the breaking of the miners' privileges by local powerholders. Bryer thought it \"a moot question\" whether the ore ran out before the fuel to smelt it. Gümüşhane's miners went where the state needed them, to Ergani, Keban and Bozkır, and orders of 1763 and 1791 dealt with them there. In 1763 King Erekle II of Kartli-Kakheti brought Chaldiot miners to the silver, lead and copper mines of Akhtala and Alaverdi in Georgia, the first Pontic communities of the Caucasus. The figure usually given, 800 households, goes back to a Greek church article of 1910.\n\n## Church, monasteries and learning\n\nSevastos Kyminitis, born at Kymina near Trebizond about 1630, taught at the Patriarchal School in Constantinople from 1671 and headed the school of Manolakis in the Phanar from 1676 to 1682. Invited home, he took charge of the Phrontisterion of Trapezous in 1683, reorganising a school that already existed, and directed it until 1689, when he left for the Princely Academy at Bucharest, where he died on 6 September 1702. Salvanou writes that merchant families such as the Velissarides and Kallivazis supported the school. It ran until November 1921. The three monasteries of Matzouka kept their Ottoman-confirmed estates and their rank as patriarchal exarchates, and in 1698 the old see of Kerasous was joined to the metropolis of Trebizond.\n\n## The derebeys\n\nIn the 18th century local dynasties ruled in the sultan's name: the Caniklizade at Samsun, among them Hacı Ali Pasha (died 1785) and Battal Hüseyin Pasha, captured by the Russians near Anapa in 1790; the Hazinedarzade, from Adjara, later at Trabzon and Gümüşhane; and the Tuzcuoğulları at Rize and Of. Bryer counts fourteen derebey families in the eastern Pontus, each largely confined to one valley. Their feuds made the countryside insecure; in 1764 Kerasous was taken after a war of 74 days by a rival lord, its derebey drowned and the town and its church ravaged. For the Christians these were decades of insecurity and forced levies, which pushed highland families to seek protection, convert or move. The last derebey wars, of 1814–1834, belong to the next chapter.\n\n## The numbers, and who gives them\n\n| What | Figure | Who gives it |\n|---|---|---|\n| Taken from Trebizond after the fall, 1461 | 1,500 children; 800 boys for the janissaries | Kritoboulos; the continuator of Chalkokondyles |\n| Trabzon city about 1486 | about 6,700 people; 19 percent Muslim | Lowry, from the register |\n| Trabzon city about 1523 | about 7,000 people; 14 percent Muslim | Lowry |\n| Trabzon city 1553 | about 6,100 people; 47 percent Muslim | Lowry |\n| Trabzon city 1583 | about 10,600 people; 54 percent Muslim, 45 percent of them converts or converts' sons | Lowry |\n| Greek households \"from Trabzon\" in Istanbul, 1540 | 279 | the Istanbul register, in Lowry |\n| Of valley | 2 percent Muslim in 1515, 11 in 1553, 24 in 1583 | the registers, in Umur and Poutouridou |\n| Of, 1879 | 8,000–10,000 Greek-speaking families, 192 of them Christian | Parcharides, a Greek estimate |\n| Mines at Gümüşhane, 1647 | 70 | Evliya Çelebi |\n| Miners taken to Akhtala, 1763 | 800 households | a Greek church article of 1910 |\n\n## Where historians disagree\n\nFor the city, the registers show state resettlement of Muslims first, then a deportation of Christians to Istanbul, then conversion. In the countryside conversion came later, from the 17th century, and is better documented in Of than in Tonya. Greek tradition makes the conversion of Of sudden and forced; Bryer & Winfield and Meeker make it gradual; the registers show slow change up to 1583, and the decisive change came after they stop. Whether the crypto-Christians were miners protecting their privileges (Bryer), people of several negotiated identities (Tzedopoulos), or, in Greek national writing, secret martyrs of the faith, is debated.\n\n## Legend and evidence\n\nBishop Alexandros-Iskender of Of, the three Greek-speaking dervishes from Maraş who are said to have converted the valley, Santa's conversion to save a pasture, Selim I or Mehmed II endowing Soumela, and the story that the Phrontisterion began at Soumela are traditions. The evidence is the register figures, the disappearance of the see of Of after 1645, the Ottoman orders of 1694–95 and 1709, Dositheos' survey of 1681, and the church documents printed by Chrysanthos.\n\n## The sources\n\nThe Ottoman tax registers of Trabzon, studied by Lowry (1977, 1981, 2009), give the city's households from about 1486 to 1583; his \"Privilege and property in Ottoman Maçuka\" (1986) treats the monasteries' estates. Umur (1951, 1956) transliterates the registers and documents of Of and Trabzon, and Poutouridou (1997) analyses those of Of. Bryer's \"The crypto-Christians of the Pontos and Consul William Gifford Palgrave of Trebizond\" (1983) uses the British consular archives, and Tzedopoulos (2009) studies the crypto-Christians and the accounts of them. Meeker (1971, 2002) and Mackridge (1987) treat Of and its Greek. Karabıçak (2021) uses the Ottoman archives on the mines, and Evliya Çelebi describes them in 1647. Chrysanthos (1933), the last metropolitan of Trebizond, gives the church's own record, with documents and manuscript notes, and Ioannidis (1870) the local Greek tradition.\n"};
